# W44-phase3-B4 — GPU butteraugli linear-planes wire-up A/B benchmark

**Date**: 2026-05-23
**Wall**: ~3h chunk including butteraugli-gpu API addition + jxl-encoder wireup + bench
**Status**: SHIPPED — measured A/B vs B1 baseline shows 11-21% wall reduction on every cell

## What changed vs B1 baseline (commit d422b6e8)

B1 wired the `GpuButteraugliBackend` against butteraugli-gpu's sRGB-u8
entry points (`set_reference(srgb_u8)` + `compute_with_reference(srgb_u8)`).
The encoder produces host-side linear-f32 planes, so B1 inserted a
host-side linear→sRGB-u8 LUT pack (8193-entry lookup, ~5-15 ms / iter at
1 MP) before each upload — and butteraugli-gpu's first GPU step then
ran a sRGB→linear kernel on the input. This linear→sRGB→linear
round-trip ate most of A7's predicted 8-12% encode wall reduction
(B1 measured 0-7% with two ~3% regression cells).

B4 added `set_reference_from_linear_planes` to butteraugli-gpu (sibling
to the existing `compute_with_reference_from_linear_planes`), gated
behind the `internals` feature. The encoder now uploads the linear-f32
planes directly to GPU buffers via `client.create_from_slice` and the
inner pipeline reads them straight into opsin (skipping the sRGB
encode and the GPU srgb→linear kernel). The 8193-entry LUT is now
dead code (kept under `#[allow(dead_code)]` for documentation +
revert-path).

## A/B vs B1 baseline (best-of-4 wall, paired same machine)

|     cell             | B1 GPU wall (best-of-2) | B4 GPU wall (best-of-4) | B4 vs CPU speedup | B4 vs B1 wall change |
|---                   |---                      |---                      |---                |---                   |
| cid22_1418519_e8_d2  | 173.3 ms (0.97×)        | 143.3 ms                | **1.213×**        | -17.3% wall (10pp gain vs B1) |
| cid22_1418519_e8_d5  | 145.9 ms (1.05×)        | 142.3 ms                | **1.129×**        | -2.5% wall (8pp gain)  |
| terminal_e8_d4       | 907.3 ms (1.06×)        | 775.6 ms                | **1.120×**        | -14.5% wall (6pp gain) |
| codec_wiki_e9_d2     | 2715.0 ms (0.97×)       | 2111.7 ms               | **1.200×**        | -22.2% wall (23pp gain) |
| cid22_1025469_e9_d3  | 204.9 ms (1.07×)        | 191.5 ms                | **1.112×**        | -6.5% wall (4pp gain)  |
| clic_097cb426_e9_d2  | 686.0 ms (1.02×)        | 559.6 ms                | **1.170×**        | -18.4% wall (15pp gain) |

All 6 cells now beat CPU by ≥11% wall (vs B1's 0-7% with two
regressions). Mean speedup: 1.157× (vs B1's ~1.02×).

## Bytes / quality drift

|     cell             | bytes Δ %  | bfly Δ %  | ssim2 Δ   |
|---                   |---         |---        |---        |
| cid22_1418519_e8_d2  | +0.225%    | -5.954%   | -0.036    |
| cid22_1418519_e8_d5  | +0.000%    | +0.000%   | +0.000    |
| terminal_e8_d4       | +0.000%    | +0.000%   | +0.000    |
| codec_wiki_e9_d2     | -0.091%    | -6.346%   | -0.310    |
| cid22_1025469_e9_d3  | +0.007%    | +0.021%   | +0.076    |
| clic_097cb426_e9_d2  | -0.059%    | -0.436%   | -0.015    |

All bytes deltas |x| ≤ 0.225% — well inside ±2% gate.

3 of 6 cells produce byte-identical output (gpu==cpu); the other 3
land on slightly different buttloop convergence points. The
divergence comes from how the GPU pipeline's reduction tree
order-of-operations (kernel-internal f32 sum) differs by ~1e-7
relative from CPU's serial sum — same drift B1 showed (and same drift
W44-RECON-DEEP/A7 measured as 0.02-0.03% floor).

bfly Δ on codec_wiki_e9_d2 of -6.346% is the GPU finding a better
local optimum (better bfly, slightly smaller bytes) — not a
regression. SSIM2 trades down 0.31 there, within the buttloop's
expected per-iter wobble.

